#!/bin/bash
# Fuzzy-find a FILE by searching text inside it, not by its filename --
# companion to the plain filename fzf popup (Super + F). Type a query, get
# back a live-updating list of files that contain it, pick one, it opens.
# This never inserts/copies the matched text itself, only the filename.
#
# Usage: find-text [all]
#   (no arg) -- plain text files only, via ripgrep. Fast.
#   all      -- also PDFs/docs/archives/images (OCR) via ripgrep-all. Slower,
#               since it has to extract text from each one before searching.

cd "$HOME" || exit 1

if [ "${1:-}" = "all" ]; then
    RG=rga
else
    RG=rg
fi

GLOBS="-g '!.git' -g '!node_modules' -g '!.cache' -g '!.npm' -g '!.cargo' -g '!.rustup' -g '!.keychain' -g '!.local/share/containers'"

selected=$(
  fzf --disabled --ansi \
    --bind "start:reload:$RG --files-with-matches --smart-case $GLOBS ''" \
    --bind "change:reload:sleep 0.1; $RG --files-with-matches --smart-case $GLOBS {q} || true" \
    --preview "$RG --color=always --smart-case $GLOBS --context 2 {q} {} 2>/dev/null | head -50" \
    --preview-window=right:50%:wrap
)

[ -z "$selected" ] && exit 0

# nvim can't meaningfully open a PDF/docx/etc -- hand those to whatever the
# desktop has associated instead (Papers for PDFs, etc). setsid + disown
# detaches it so this popup's terminal can close instead of staying attached
# to whatever xdg-open launches (same reason theme-switch uses setsid and
# hyprpaper-init uses nohup/disown elsewhere in this repo). The short sleep
# afterward is load-bearing, not padding: there's a narrow window right after
# forking where the child can still get killed by this terminal closing
# before the detach fully lands -- confirmed by testing, it fails silently
# without this.
case "$(file --mime-type -b "$selected")" in
    text/*) nvim "$selected" ;;
    *)      setsid xdg-open "$selected" >/dev/null 2>&1 & disown; sleep 0.3 ;;
esac
